# Circuit editing

Writing to an already-wired brain at the level of individual connections, instead of
blanket stimulation (DBS, optogenetic drive of a whole cell type) or lesioning. Three
families so far: designer electrical synapses, synthetic synaptic organizers, and
activity-gated (immediate-early-gene) tagging that restricts any of the above to the
cells or spines that were recently active.

[[!toc levels=5]]

# LinCx: designer electrical synapses

"Long-term Integration of Circuits using connexins" (LinCx), from Kafui Dzirasa's lab
at Duke. Two connexins from white perch, Cx34.7 and Cx35, carry mutated docking motifs
so that the hemichannels pair only with each other and not with host connexins. Express
Cx34.7(M1) in the presynaptic population and Cx35(M1) in the postsynaptic population and
you get a tethered electrical synapse that forms only where those two cell populations
already touch.

[Long-term editing of brain circuits using an engineered electrical synapse](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10501-y)
(2026), open version: <https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13372691/>

Specificity comes from two independent sources stacked together, not just the promoter:

* cell-type/promoter specificity of each AAV (CaMKII, hSyn-DIO plus a Cre driver line), and
* the anatomical projection/contact pattern itself.

Worked example from that paper: AAV-CaMKII-Cx34.7(M1) into orbitofrontal cortex, so
excitatory OFC projection neurons express one hemichannel, and AAV-hSyn-DIO-Cx35(M1) into
striatum of PV-Cre mice, restricting the other hemichannel to striatal PV+ interneurons.
OFC axon terminals carrying Cx34.7(M1) encounter Cx35(M1)-expressing PV cells in striatum,
so electrical coupling is added at that projection-defined and cell-type-defined interface
only.

## LinCx applications so far

[Precision editing of an aggression-encoding network relay suppresses violent action in mice](https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2022.12.07.519272v5)
-- "an aggressive internal state in mice is encoded by a widespread brain network ...
organized by prominent and synchronized electrical oscillations that relay through the
medial prefrontal cortex ... Finally, we applied 'Long-term Integration of Circuits using
connexins' (LinCx) to selectively edit a key circuit within the network from the medial
prefrontal cortex to the nucleus accumbens. Editing this circuit durably suppressed violent
action while leaving non-aggressive social behavior intact."

[Synaptic editing of frontostriatal circuitry prevents excessive grooming in SAPAP3-deficient mice](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11974874/)
(2025) -- LinCx used to "deploy a designer electrical synapse to edit a single class of
chemical synapses in a genetic mouse model of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Editing
these synapses in juvenile mice normalized circuit function and prevented the emergence of
pathological repetitive behavior in adulthood". Note the window: juvenile prophylaxis, not
adult reversal.

## Caveats of electrical coupling as an engineering primitive

* It cannot grow a connection where there is no apposition. Connexins do not extend an axon
  across a gap or a lesion; LinCx edits circuits that already exist.
* No gain. A gap junction is a resistive divider, so coupling does not amplify; a single
  junction will not drive a postsynaptic cell to threshold.
* Low-pass filtering: fast spike waveforms are attenuated far more than slow depolarizations.
* Non-rectifying junctions are bidirectional and synchronizing, which in a motor pool is a
  recipe for spasticity/clonus rather than useful control.
* Conductance is fixed at expression time, not a tunable knob, and not activity-dependent
  unless you make it so (see IEG section below).

For [[paralysis|neurosurgery]] this means LinCx is plausible only where spared relays exist
(incomplete injury, propriospinal or interneuron detours), not after complete transection,
and probably needs many contacts rather than one bridge.

# Synthetic synaptic organizers

[A synthetic synaptic organizer protein restores glutamatergic neuronal circuits](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7116145/)
(2021) -- "we designed and characterized CPTX, a synthetic synaptic organizer combining
structural elements from cerebellin-1 and neuronal pentraxin-1. CPTX can interact with
presynaptic neurexins and postsynaptic AMPA-type ionotropic glutamate receptors and induced
the formation of excitatory synapses both in vitro and in vivo. CPTX restored synaptic
functions, motor coordination, spatial and contextual memories, and locomotion in mouse
models for cerebellar ataxia, Alzheimer's disease and spinal cord injury, respectively.
Thus, CPTX represents a prototype for structure-guided biologics that can efficiently repair
or remodel neuronal circuits."

Complementary to LinCx: CPTX builds a real chemical synapse (with gain and plasticity) but
its specificity is whatever the injected protein diffuses to, whereas LinCx has genetic and
projection specificity but no gain.

# Activity-dependent (immediate-early-gene) targeting of synapses

The missing combination: use Arc/Fos regulatory elements so the circuit-editing payload only
lands on recently active neurons or recently potentiated spines.

[Activity-dependent expression of Channelrhodopsin at neuronal synapses](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-017-01699-7)
(2017) -- SynActive. "a hybrid RNA/protein approach to regulate the expression of a
light-sensitive membrane channel at activated synapses, enabling selective tagging of
potentiated spines following the encoding of a novel context in the hippocampus. This
approach can be used to map potentiated synapses in the brain and will make it possible to
re-activate the neuron only at previously activated synapses". Gobbo et al. used regulatory
sequences from Arc mRNA plus postsynaptic targeting sequences.

[Labelling and optical erasure of synaptic memory traces in the motor cortex](https://www.nature.com/articles/nature15257)
(2015) -- AS-PaRac1 (activated synapse targeting photoactivatable Rac1) "can label recently
potentiated spines specifically, and induce the selective shrinkage of AS-PaRac1-containing
spines ... The acquired motor learning was disrupted by the optical shrinkage of the
potentiated spines." Exploits Arc-related dendritic targeting / local translation plus PSD
targeting.

[Interregional synaptic maps among engram cells underlie memory formation](https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aas9204)
(Choi et al. 2018) -- Fos-rtTA driving dual-eGRASP to label engram-to-engram synapses. Same
split-protein-across-the-cleft, two-population logic as LinCx, but the payload is a
fluorophore instead of a conductance.

## TODO / open experiment

Nobody appears to have published IEG-gated LinCx: the Nature 2026 work used CaMKII, hSyn and
Ef1a-DIO plus Cre, no activity-dependent promoter. The obvious build is Cx34.7(M1) under
Fos-tTA/TetTag, TRAP2 (Fos-CreERT2) or E-SARE, with Cx35(M1) delivered by projection/cell-type
targeting, i.e. swap eGRASP for the connexin pair. Two known problems:

* timescale mismatch: IEG tagging windows are hours, while functional electrical coupling
  needs days of expression to accumulate;
* if both sides are IEG-tagged you are betting that the two populations are co-active *and*
  physically apposed, which Fos alone does not guarantee. Better to tag one side and use
  anatomy for the other.

See also the constitutive-overexpression trap: Arc/Egr1/Fos effectors expressed constitutively
degrade memory by destroying ensemble sparseness, so the activity-gated promoter is not an
optional nicety.

# Enhancement ideas (speculative)

Circuit editing as a cognitive intervention means increasing the efficiency of signal
propagation along a connection that already exists, rather than adding new projections (which
is what you would actually want, for dimensionality).

Candidate target: frontoparietal structural connectivity between left rostrolateral prefrontal
cortex (RLPFC) and left inferior parietal cortex, via superior longitudinal fasciculus, where
childhood structural connectivity longitudinally predicts later reasoning ability:
[Frontoparietal structural connectivity in childhood predicts development of functional connectivity and reasoning ability](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28821657/)
(Wendelken et al., J Neurosci 2017, <https://doi.org/10.1523/JNEUROSCI.3726-16.2017>), with
predecessors [Ferrer et al. 2013](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3801426/) and
[Wendelken et al. 2016](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4816790/). Caution: that
evidence is developmental, longitudinal and correlational, so "add coupling between RLPFC and
IPC in an adult" is an extrapolation, and electrical coupling adds synchrony, not bandwidth.

A failed idea worth recording: editing cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) fibers passing
through the anterior limb of the internal capsule (ALIC) to *increase* OCD-like tendency. The
neuroanatomy does not support it in that form; what is actually established there is
disruption of that circuit by capsulotomy/DBS, not a known gain-of-function write. The
SAPAP3 frontostriatal paper above is the version that is actually demonstrated.

